/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_BuildGuide_PSoC
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildPSoCTarget Experimental Target Profile
 *
 * Foundation provides this experimental target alias:
 *
 * @code
 * PSoC 5LP
 * ARM GCC
 * Cortex-M3
 * Soft Float
 * @endcode
 *
 * Target preset:
 *
 * @code
 * psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft
 * @endcode
 *
 * It inherits `arm_none_eabi_cortex_m3_soft`; today the alias changes package
 * identity, not compiler flags. It therefore proves that Foundation can produce
 * a Cortex-M3/Thumb/soft-float archive, but does not by itself prove PSoC
 * Creator compatibility or execution on a PSoC device.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildPSoCWhy Why not every chip
 *
 * Foundation should not initially generate one distribution per PSoC chip.
 *
 * The minimum useful split is:
 *
 * - toolchain,
 * - CPU core,
 * - ABI/FPU mode,
 * - configuration.
 *
 * Since Foundation is mostly portable C++ and does not depend on chip
 * registers, a compatible Cortex-M3 soft-float build may eventually cover a
 * CY8CKIT-059 consumer. The preset currently produces a generic Cortex-M3
 * soft-float static archive. It does not provide PSoC Creator startup code,
 * generated components, linker script, device headers, or project metadata.
 *
 * The consuming PSoC project must use a compatible Arm GNU compiler/runtime and
 * the same Thumb, Cortex-M3, and soft-float ABI. Compiler compatibility must be
 * validated with the exact PSoC Creator/toolchain version used by the product.
 * This is especially important for the Foundation C++17 contract: PSoC Creator
 * 4.3 bundles GCC 5.4-2016-q2, whose C++17 mode was experimental and whose
 * C++17 ABI was not yet stable. PSoC Creator also exposes an `ARM GCC Generic`
 * integration for a separately installed compiler, but Foundation has not yet
 * validated that path.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildPSoCStatus 1.x Status Decision
 *
 * PSoC 5LP remains `EXPERIMENTAL` in Foundation 1.x. `VAL-009` records this as
 * a deliberate status decision rather than unfinished generic compilation.
 * Promotion requires all of the following evidence from the same environment:
 *
 * - an exact PSoC 5LP part or board and PSoC Creator version;
 * - a C++17-capable selected ARM GCC toolchain and recorded version;
 * - a clean Foundation package built with matching CPU, ABI, runtime, and
 *   compiler compatibility;
 * - a real PSoC Creator consumer that links `Foundation::Foundation` or the
 *   installed archive without source-tree leakage; and
 * - successful programming and execution of representative checks on hardware.
 *
 * Official references:
 *
 * - <a href="https://www.infineon.com/evaluation-board/CY8CKIT-059">Infineon
 *   CY8CKIT-059 product page</a>;
 * - <a href="https://www.infineon.com/assets/row/public/documents/cross-divisions/44/infineon-psoc-creator-4.3-release-notes-usermanual-en.pdf">PSoC Creator 4.3
 *   release notes</a>; and
 * - <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/projects/cxx-status.html#cxx17">GCC C++17
 *   implementation status</a>.
 *
 * @section FoundationBuildPSoCExport Export
 *
 * Example:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft
 * @endcode
 *
 * or:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft
 * @endcode
 *
 * Output:
 *
 * @code
 * dist/psoc5lp_armgcc_cortex_m3_soft/
 * @endcode
 *
 * Inspect the Release archive with `arm-none-eabi-objdump -f`. Treat the output
 * as experimental until it is linked into a PSoC Creator firmware using the
 * exact product toolchain and executed on hardware. Do not use host CTest or
 * generic cross-compilation as proof of PSoC compatibility.
 */
